# Accepted Trivy findings.
#
# Inline "trivy:ignore" comments were tried first and are not sufficient here:
# several of these are raised against terraform-aws-modules source downloaded
# into .terraform/, which is not ours to annotate. A central file also keeps
# every accepted risk in one reviewable place with a reason and an expiry, so
# an exception has to be re-argued rather than quietly inherited forever.
#
# Rule: nothing goes in this file without a justification and a date.

# Load balancer is internet-facing.
# This is a public web service; an internet-facing load balancer is the
# requirement. Reviewed 2026-09-16.
AVD-AWS-0053

# HTTP listener does not redirect to HTTPS.
# Conditional, not absolute: port 80 forwards directly only when no ACM
# certificate is supplied, which is the case in environments with no registered
# domain. Set certificate_arn and this becomes a 301 to a TLS 1.3 listener.
# See docs/adr/0007-tls-termination.md. Reviewed 2026-09-16.
exp:2026-12-31
AVD-AWS-0054

# Unrestricted egress from the ECS task security group.
# Tasks must reach ECR, CloudWatch Logs, Secrets Manager and RDS, and the
# service endpoints behind those have no stable address range. Ingress is
# already restricted to the load balancer alone, and the subnet has no inbound
# path from the internet. Closed properly by VPC endpoints plus prefix lists,
# which is on the roadmap. Reviewed 2026-09-16.
exp:2026-12-31
AVD-AWS-0104

# S3 buckets use SSE-S3 rather than a customer-managed key.
# For the ALB access-log bucket this is not fixable: log delivery supports
# SSE-S3 only, and configuring SSE-KMS silently stops delivery rather than
# failing. For the state bucket it is a deliberate deferral -- a CMK adds a key
# whose deletion makes every state file unreadable, which is a worse failure
# than the one it prevents until key administration is owned. Reviewed
# 2026-09-16.
AVD-AWS-0132

# SNS topic uses the AWS-managed key rather than a customer-managed key.
# The topic is encrypted; the scanner wants a CMK specifically. Same reasoning
# as AVD-AWS-0132: a CMK here buys key-policy control we have no requirement
# for yet, at the cost of another key to administer. Reviewed 2026-09-16.
AVD-AWS-0136

# CVE-2025-47273 -- path traversal in setuptools' PackageIndex.
#
# Accepted, and the reasoning matters more than the acceptance.
#
# Reachability: the vulnerable code path is PackageIndex, which runs only when
# setuptools is used to download packages from an index. The runtime container
# never invokes setuptools: the application is started by uvicorn from
# /opt/venv, installs nothing, and runs as a non-root user with a read-only root
# filesystem. There is no path from a request to this code.
#
# Remediation attempted, and what was learned: the declared dependency is pinned
# to setuptools 84.0.0, which is patched, and the virtualenv demonstrably
# contains that version. The scanner nonetheless reports 70.3.0, so the copy it
# finds is not the one requirements.txt governs. System pip and setuptools are
# removed from the runtime image (worth doing regardless -- a package installer
# has no business in a running container), and the finding persists, so it is
# somewhere the removal does not reach.
#
# This is exactly the case VEX exists for, and Trivy's own output recommends it.
# A VEX statement asserting not_affected with justification
# vulnerable_code_not_in_execute_path is the correct long-term fix and is on the
# roadmap; this entry is the interim, with the same reasoning written down.
#
# Reviewed 2026-09-16. Revisit when the base image ships a patched setuptools,
# at which point this entry should be deleted rather than renewed.
exp:2026-12-31
CVE-2025-47273
